\A{ppk} PPK file format

This appendix documents the file format used by PuTTY and KiTTY++ to
store private keys. In KiTTY++, kageant, kittygen and kittygen-cli
read and write it. kitty.exe, klink, kscp and ksftp read it.

KiTTY++ can also protect a key with Windows Hello. That protection
does not change the PPK format. It adds a separate file beside the
key, documented in \k{ppk-hello}.

In this appendix, binary data structures are described using data type
representations such as \cq{uint32}, \cq{string} and \cq{mpint} as
used in the SSH protocol standards themselves. These are defined
authoritatively by
\W{https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4251#section-5}{RFC 4251 section 5},
\q{Data Type Representations Used in the SSH Protocols}.

\H{ppk-overview} Overview

A PPK file stores a private key, and the corresponding public key.
Both are contained in the same file.

The file format can be completely unencrypted, or it can encrypt the
private key. The \e{public} key is stored in cleartext in both cases.
(This enables PuTTY and KiTTY++ to send the public key to an SSH server to see
whether it will accept it, and not bother prompting for the passphrase
unless the server answers yes.)

When the key file is encrypted, the encryption key is derived from a
passphrase. An encrypted PPK file is also tamper-proofed using a MAC
(authentication code), also derived from the same passphrase. The MAC
protects the encrypted private key data, but it also covers the
cleartext parts of the file. So you can't edit the public half of the
key without invalidating the MAC and causing the key file as a whole
to become useless.

This MAC protects the key file against active cryptographic attacks in
which the public half of a key pair is modified in a controlled way
that allows an attacker to deduce information about the private half
from the resulting corrupted signatures. Any attempt to do that to a
PPK file should be reliably caught by the MAC failing to validate.

(Such an attack would only be useful if the key file was stored in a
location where the attacker could modify it without also having full
access to the process that you type passphrases into. But that's not
impossible; for example, if your home directory was on a network file
server, then the file server's administrator could access the key file
but not processes on the client machine.)

The MAC also covers the \e{comment} on the key. This stops an attacker
from swapping keys with each other and editing the comments to
disguise the fact. As a consequence, PuTTYgen and kittygen cannot edit the comment
on a key unless you decrypt the key with your passphrase first.

(The circumstances in which \e{that} attack would be useful are even
more restricted. One example might be that the different keys trigger
specific actions on the server you're connecting to and one of those
actions is more useful to the attacker than the other. But once you
have a MAC at all, it's no extra effort to make it cover as much as
possible, and usually sensible.)

\H{ppk-outer} Outer layer

The outer layer of a PPK file is text-based. The PuTTY and KiTTY++ tools will
always use LF line termination when writing PPK files, but will
tolerate CR+LF and CR-only on input.

The first few lines identify it as a PPK, and give some initial data
about what's stored in it and how. They look like this:

\c PuTTY-User-Key-File-version: algorithm-name
\e                     bbbbbbb  bbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c Encryption: encryption-type
\e             bbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c Comment: key-comment-string
\e          bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb

\s{version} is a decimal number giving the version number of the file
format itself. The current file format version is 3.

\s{algorithm-name} is the SSH protocol identifier for the public key
algorithm that this key is used for (such as \cq{ssh-dss} or
\cq{ecdsa-sha2-nistp384}).

\s{encryption-type} indicates whether this key is stored encrypted,
and if so, by what method. Currently the only supported encryption
types are \cq{aes256-cbc} and \cq{none}.

\s{key-comment-string} is a free text field giving the comment. This
can contain any byte values other than 13 and 10 (CR and LF).

The next part of the file gives the public key. This is stored
unencrypted but base64-encoded
(\W{https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4648}{RFC 4648}), and is preceded
by a header line stating how many lines of base64 data are shown,
looking like this:

\c Public-Lines: number-of-lines
\e               bbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c that many lines of base64 data
\e bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb

The base64-encoded data in this blob is formatted in exactly the same
way as an SSH public key sent over the wire in the SSH protocol
itself. That is also the same format as the base64 data stored in
OpenSSH's \c{authorized_keys} file, except that in a PPK file the
base64 data is split across multiple lines. But if you remove the
newlines from the middle of this section, the resulting base64 blob is
in the right format to go in an \c{authorized_keys} line.

If the key is encrypted (i.e. \s{encryption-type} is not \cq{none}),
then the next thing that appears is a sequence of lines specifying how
the keys for encrypting the file are to be derived from the
passphrase:

\c Key-Derivation: argon2-flavour
\e                 bbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c Argon2-Memory: decimal-integer
\e                bbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c Argon2-Passes: decimal-integer
\e                bbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c Argon2-Parallelism: decimal-integer
\e                     bbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c Argon2-Salt: hex-string
\e              bbbbbbbbbb

\s{argon2-flavour} is one of the identifiers \cq{Argon2d},
\cq{Argon2i} or \cq{Argon2id}, all describing variants of the Argon2
password-hashing function.

The three integer values are used as parameters for Argon2, which
allows you to configure the amount of memory used (in Kbyte), the number
of passes of the algorithm to run (to tune its running time), and the
degree of parallelism required by the hash function. The salt is
decoded into a sequence of binary bytes and used as an additional
input to Argon2. (It is chosen randomly when the key file is written,
so that a guessing attack can't be mounted in parallel against
multiple key files.)

The next part of the file gives the private key. This is
base64-encoded in the same way:

\c Private-Lines: number-of-lines
\e                bbbbbbbbbbbbbbb
\c that many lines of base64 data
\e bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb

The binary data represented in this base64 blob may be encrypted,
depending on the \e{encryption-type} field in the key file header
shown above:

\b If \s{encryption-type} is \cq{none}, then this data is stored in
plain text.

\b If \s{encryption-type} is \cq{aes256-cbc}, then this data is
encrypted using AES, with a 256-bit key length, in the CBC cipher
mode. The key and initialisation vector are derived from the
passphrase: see \k{ppk-keys}.

\lcont{

In order to encrypt the private key data with AES, it must be a
multiple of 16 bytes (the AES cipher block length). This is achieved
by appending random padding to the data before encrypting it. When
decoding it after decryption, the random data can be ignored: the
internal structure of the data is enough to show you when you've
reached the end of the meaningful part.

}

Unlike public keys, the binary encoding of private keys is not
specified at all in the SSH standards. See \k{ppk-privkeys} for
details of the private key format for each key type supported by
PuTTY and KiTTY++.

The final thing in the key file is the MAC:

\c Private-MAC: hex-mac-data
\e              bbbbbbbbbbbb

\s{hex-mac-data} is a hexadecimal-encoded value, 64 digits long (i.e.
32 bytes), generated using the HMAC-SHA-256 algorithm with the
following binary data as input:

\b \cw{string}: the \s{algorithm-name} header field.

\b \cw{string}: the \s{encryption-type} header field.

\b \cw{string}: the \s{key-comment-string} header field.

\b \cw{string}: the binary public key data, as decoded from the base64
lines after the \cq{Public-Lines} header.

\b \cw{string}: the plaintext of the binary private key data, as
decoded from the base64 lines after the \cq{Private-Lines} header. If
that data was stored encrypted, then the decrypted version of it is
used in this MAC preimage, \e{including} the random padding mentioned
above.

The MAC key is derived from the passphrase: see \k{ppk-keys}.

\H{ppk-privkeys} Private key encodings

This section describes the private key format for each key type
supported by PuTTY and KiTTY++.

Because the PPK format also contains the public key (and both public
and private key are protected by the same MAC to ensure they can't be
made inconsistent), there is no need for the private key section of
the file to repeat data from the public section. So some of these
formats are very short.

In all cases, a decoding application can begin reading from the start
of the decrypted private key data, and know when it has read all that
it needs. This allows random padding after the meaningful data to be
safely ignored.

\S{ppk-privkey-rsa} RSA

RSA keys are stored using an \s{algorithm-name} of \cq{ssh-rsa}. (Keys
stored like this are also used by the updated RSA signature schemes
that use hashes other than SHA-1.)

The public key data has already provided the key modulus and the
public encoding exponent. The private data stores:

\b \cw{mpint}: the private decoding exponent of the key.

\b \cw{mpint}: one prime factor \e{p} of the key.

\b \cw{mpint}: the other prime factor \e{q} of the key. (RSA keys
stored in this format are expected to have exactly two prime factors.)

\b \cw{mpint}: the multiplicative inverse of \e{q} modulo \e{p}.

\S{ppk-privkey-dsa} DSA

DSA keys are stored using an \s{algorithm-name} of \cq{ssh-dss}.

The public key data has already provided the key parameters (the large
prime \e{p}, the small prime \e{q} and the group generator \e{g}), and
the public key \e{y}. The private key stores:

\b \cw{mpint}: the private key \e{x}, which is the discrete logarithm
of \e{y} in the group generated by \e{g} mod \e{p}.

\S{ppk-privkey-ecdsa} NIST elliptic-curve keys

\i{NIST} elliptic-curve keys are stored using one of the following
\s{algorithm-name} values, each corresponding to a different elliptic
curve and key size:

\b \cq{ecdsa-sha2-nistp256}

\b \cq{ecdsa-sha2-nistp384}

\b \cq{ecdsa-sha2-nistp521}

The public key data has already provided the public elliptic curve
point. The private key stores:

\b \cw{mpint}: the private exponent, which is the discrete log of the
public point.

\S{ppk-privkey-eddsa} EdDSA elliptic-curve keys (Ed25519 and Ed448)

EdDSA elliptic-curve keys are stored using one of the following
\s{algorithm-name} values, each corresponding to a different elliptic
curve and key size:

\b \cq{ssh-ed25519}

\b \cq{ssh-ed448}

The public key data has already provided the public elliptic curve
point. The private key stores:

\b \cw{mpint}: the private exponent, which is the discrete log of the
public point.

\H{ppk-keys} Key derivation

When a key file is encrypted, there are three pieces of key material
that need to be computed from the passphrase:

\b the key for the symmetric cipher used to encrypt the private key

\b the initialisation vector for that cipher encryption

\b the key for the MAC.

If \s{encryption-type} is \cq{aes256-cbc}, then the symmetric cipher
key is 32 bytes long, and the initialisation vector is 16 bytes (one
cipher block). The length of the MAC key is also chosen to be 32
bytes.

If \s{encryption-type} is \cq{none}, then all three of these pieces of
data have zero length. (The MAC is still generated and checked in the
key file format, but it has a zero-length key.)

If the amount of key material required is not zero, then the
passphrase is fed to the Argon2 key derivation function, in whichever
mode is described in the \cq{Key-Derivation} header in the key file,
with parameters derived from the various
\q{\cw{Argon2-}\e{Parameter}\cw{:}} headers.

(If the key is unencrypted, then all those headers are omitted, and
Argon2 is not run at all.)

Argon2 takes two extra string inputs in addition to the passphrase and
the salt: a secret key, and some \q{associated data}. In PPK's use of
Argon2, these are both set to the empty string.

The \q{tag length} parameter to Argon2 (i.e. the amount of data it is
instructed to output) is set to the sum of the lengths of all of the data
items required, i.e. (cipher key length + IV length + MAC key length).
The output data is interpreted as the concatenation of the cipher key,
the IV and the MAC key, in that order.

So, for \cq{aes256-cbc}, the tag length will be 32+16+32\_=\_80 bytes;
of the 80 bytes of output data, the first 32 bytes are used as the
256-bit AES key, the next 16 as the CBC IV, and the final 32 bytes as
the HMAC-SHA-256 key.

\H{ppk-hello} Windows Hello protection: the \c{.hello} file

This section documents an addition made by KiTTY++. It is not part of
the PPK format. A key protected with Windows Hello is an ordinary
encrypted PPK file as described above. The material that opens it is
kept in a second file beside it, the \e{sidecar}.

\S{ppk-hello-overview} Overview

To protect a key, KiTTY++ generates a random 32-byte secret with the
system random number generator. The PPK file is saved with the
\e{printed secret} (the text form of that secret, see
\k{ppk-hello-printed}) as its passphrase. The sidecar holds the same
secret, encrypted separately for each way of opening the key. This
section calls each such encrypted copy a \e{door}. There are three
kinds:

\b A \e{W door} opens through Windows Hello with a passkey (a WebAuthn
platform credential) of one Windows account on one computer. A sidecar
can hold several W doors, one for each account and computer that was
added.

\b An \e{H door} opens through Windows Hello with a Windows Hello key
credential (\cw{KeyCredentialManager}). KiTTY++ writes an H door only
where the passkey route is unavailable.

\b An \e{R door} opens with a typed recovery passphrase or with a
recovery code. A sidecar can hold several R doors.

The printed secret is not stored in the sidecar. It is the passphrase
of the PPK file itself, so it opens the key in any program that reads
PPK files, with or without the sidecar.

The PPK file is written with the PPK parameters of the program that
writes it (kageant uses the defaults: format version 3 with Argon2id).
Its encryption type is always \cq{aes256-cbc}, because the printed
secret is never empty.

The KiTTY++ programs handle protected keys as follows:

\b kageant writes a protected \e{copy} of a key. It never rewrites the
source file. The default file name of the copy is the source file name
with \c{-hello} inserted before the extension, for example
\c{id-hello.ppk} for \c{id.ppk}.

\b kittygen writes a protected key when it saves a key with Windows
Hello protection selected. Its \q{Windows Hello doors} dialog adds or
removes W doors.

\b kittygen-cli writes a protected key with only an R door (see
\k{ppk-hello-cli}).

\b kageant, kittygen and kitty.exe read the sidecar. They try Windows
Hello first. Their passphrase prompts accept the other doors (see
\k{ppk-hello-open}).

\b klink, kscp and ksftp do not read the sidecar. To them a protected
key is an ordinary encrypted PPK file, which the printed secret opens
at their passphrase prompt. kitty.exe does not pass a protected key
file to kscp, ksftp or WinSCP. Such transfers use the key through
kageant.

\S{ppk-hello-file} File name and outer layer

The file name of the sidecar is the full file name of the key file
with \c{.hello} appended, in the same directory. For \c{id-hello.ppk}
it is \c{id-hello.ppk.hello}. The programs find the sidecar by this
file name alone. Neither file records the name or location of the
other.

The sidecar is a text file holding a single line, the \e{container}
(\k{ppk-hello-container}). It is ASCII text terminated by CR+LF (unlike
the PPK file, which uses LF). Readers remove trailing CR, LF, space and
tab characters. A sidecar that is empty, 1 MiB or larger, or whose
text is not a valid container is treated as unreadable.

\S{ppk-hello-container} The container

The container is the marker \cq{HELLOK1:} followed by one or more
fields, separated by \cq{.} characters:

\c HELLOK1:field.field.field
\e         bbbbb bbbbb bbbbb

The first character of each field gives its kind: \cq{W}, \cq{H} or
\cq{R}. Readers skip fields of any other kind, which leaves room for
later additions. W and R fields can occur more than once. A container
is valid when it holds at least one W, H or R field. Readers locate
fields by kind, not by position.

Binary values in the fields are base64-encoded as in the PPK file
(\W{https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4648}{RFC 4648}), without line
breaks. The base64 alphabet contains neither \cq{.} nor \cq{,}, so
those characters work as separators.

\S{ppk-hello-blob} The wrapped secret

Every door holds the secret as a 60-byte \e{blob}:

\b 12 bytes: a random nonce.

\b 32 bytes: the secret, encrypted with AES-256 in GCM mode.

\b 16 bytes: the GCM authentication tag.

The encryption is standard AES-256-GCM with the nonce as its 96-bit
IV. The additional authenticated data is the 8 ASCII bytes
\cq{HELLOK1:}, colon included. The 32-byte AES key is the door's
\e{key-encryption key} (KEK). Each door type derives its KEK
differently, as described below. Every blob gets a fresh nonce from
the system random number generator. A reader verifies the tag before
it decrypts. A wrong KEK or a modified blob fails that check.

\S{ppk-hello-w} W field: passkey door

\c Wcredential-id,blob
\e  bbbbbbbbbbbbb bbbb
\c Wcredential-id,blob,owner
\e  bbbbbbbbbbbbb bbbb bbbbb

\s{credential-id} is the base64 encoding of the WebAuthn credential ID
of the passkey. \s{blob} is the base64 encoding of the wrapped secret.
\s{owner} is optional. It is the base64 encoding of a text label for
the Windows account and computer that wrote the door, built from the
\cw{USERDOMAIN} environment variable, the user name and the computer
name. Readers only display that label. They select a W door by its
credential ID.

The passkey is a credential of the Windows platform authenticator,
used through \cw{webauthn.dll} (WebAuthn API version 6 or later). Its
relying party ID is \cq{kapper.net kitty++} and its user ID is the
ASCII bytes \cq{kitty.hello.keys}. It is created with the CTAP
\cq{hmac-secret} extension, platform attachment and user verification
required. Before it creates a passkey, KiTTY++ searches the platform
credential list for that relying party ID and uses the first
credential found. It creates a new passkey only when that search finds
none.

The KEK of a W door is the SHA-256 hash of the 32-byte
\cq{hmac-secret} output of a WebAuthn assertion with the passkey. The
assertion requires user verification, which is the Windows Hello
prompt. Its salt is fixed: the 21 ASCII bytes
\cq{KiTTY PRF KEK salt v1} followed by 11 zero bytes, passed to the
authenticator as a raw salt.

To open a container, a reader looks up the passkey of the current
Windows account in the platform credential list. It tries only a W
field with the same credential ID. W fields of other accounts or
computers are never tried.

\S{ppk-hello-h} H field: key credential door

\c Hblob
\e  bbbb

\s{blob} is the base64 encoding of the wrapped secret. Its KEK comes
from a Windows Hello key credential with the name
\cq{kapper.net.KiTTY}, used through the WinRT class
\cw{Windows.Security.Credentials.KeyCredentialManager}. KiTTY++ signs
the fixed ASCII string

\c KiTTY Windows Hello KEK challenge, container format HELLOK1

with \cw{RequestSignAsync}, which shows the Windows Hello prompt. The
KEK is the SHA-256 hash of the signature. This derivation depends on
the credential producing the same signature for the same input every
time. The KiTTY++ source documents this as a property of the RSA
PKCS#1 v1.5 signatures these credentials make.

When it writes an H door, KiTTY++ opens the existing credential of
that name. It creates the credential (with the \cw{FailIfExists}
option) only when none exists. It never replaces an existing
credential. When it reads an H door, it opens the existing credential
and never creates one. Readers use the first H field of a container.

\S{ppk-hello-r} R field: recovery door

\c Rmemory,passes,parallelism,salt,blob
\e  bbbbbb bbbbbb bbbbbbbbbbb bbbb bbbb
\c Rmemory,passes,parallelism,salt,blob,c
\e  bbbbbb bbbbbb bbbbbbbbbbb bbbb bbbb

\s{memory}, \s{passes} and \s{parallelism} are decimal Argon2
parameters, with memory in Kbyte as in \k{ppk-outer}. \s{salt} is the
base64 encoding of a 16-byte random salt. \s{blob} is the base64
encoding of the wrapped secret. A trailing \cq{,c} marks a door keyed
on a recovery code (\k{ppk-hello-printed}) rather than on a typed
recovery passphrase. Readers ignore any other trailing tag. The two
forms are identical in their cryptography.

The KEK of an R door is the 32-byte output of Argon2id over the
recovery passphrase or recovery code text, with the salt and
parameters recorded in the field. The Argon2 secret key and associated
data are empty strings. KiTTY++ currently writes 65536 Kbyte of
memory, 3 passes and a parallelism of 4. A reader uses the recorded
values, so a later change of these values does not lock out older
sidecars. It checks the recorded values for legality before it runs
Argon2 and refuses the door if they fail that check.

A reader tries the typed text on every R field in turn. Only the door
keyed on that text passes the tag check.

The R door is optional. In kageant, the recovery passphrase is either
the existing passphrase of the source key or a separately typed one.
kittygen uses the passphrase typed in its passphrase fields. Both
write a sidecar without an R door only after a warning, when the user
chooses to give no recovery passphrase.

\S{ppk-hello-printed} Printed secret and recovery code

The \e{printed secret} is the 32-byte secret as 64 upper-case
hexadecimal digits in 8 groups of 8 digits, followed by a check group
of 4 digits. The check group holds the first 2 bytes of the SHA-256
hash of the secret. The groups are separated by \cq{-}. This text is
the literal passphrase of the PPK file. Readers accept either case
and ignore dashes, spaces and tabs. They verify the check group. They
convert accepted text back into the exact form above before they use
it as a passphrase.

The \e{recovery code} is an optional printout that opens the key only
together with its sidecar. kageant and kittygen offer it as an
alternative to the printed secret. With that choice, the printed
secret is not displayed. The passphrase of the PPK file is then shown
nowhere and written to no file. The code is 16 random bytes,
written as \cq{KRC1}, then 8 groups of 4 upper-case hexadecimal
digits, then a check group of 2 digits holding the first byte of the
SHA-256 hash of the 16 bytes. The groups are separated by \cq{-}.
Readers require the \cq{KRC1} prefix. They accept either case and
ignore dashes, spaces and tabs. They verify the check group. The code
text in the exact form above is the passphrase of an R door marked
\cq{,c}.

\S{ppk-hello-open} Opening a protected key

kageant, kittygen and kitty.exe treat a key file as protected when a
sidecar for it exists. They open it in these steps:

\n Windows Hello. If the container holds a W field whose credential ID
matches the passkey of the current account, the reader derives that
door's KEK (a Windows Hello prompt) and opens it. Otherwise, if the
container holds an H field, the reader derives the KEK of the H door
from the existing key credential. The secret it recovers is converted
into the printed secret, which then decrypts the PPK file.

\n The passphrase prompt. If the Windows Hello step fails, is
cancelled or finds no door for the current account, the reader shows
its passphrase prompt. When the container holds no door for the
current account, kageant first displays a message that lists the
owners of the doors in the container. The prompt accepts these forms
of text:

\lcont{

\b A recovery code (text with the \cq{KRC1} prefix). It opens the R
door keyed on it.

\b Text in the printed-secret format. The reader first tries it as the
passphrase of an R door, because recovery codes written by earlier
KiTTY++ builds had that format. If no R door opens, it uses the text
as the PPK passphrase.

\b Any other text. The reader tries it as the recovery passphrase of
each R door. If no R door opens, it uses the text unchanged as the PPK
passphrase.

}

\n Decryption. The PPK file is decrypted with the resulting
passphrase as described in \k{ppk-keys}. If a secret from the sidecar
does not decrypt the PPK file (for example a sidecar that belongs to a
different key), the reader falls back to the passphrase prompt.

\S{ppk-hello-doors} Adding and removing doors

A W door for a further Windows account or computer is added from that
account, after the key was opened through another door. kageant
offers this after an R door opened the key. kittygen does it from its
\q{Windows Hello doors} dialog, which accepts the recovery passphrase,
a recovery code or the printed secret. Adding a door needs the printed
secret and a Windows Hello prompt, plus the passkey creation flow of
Windows when the current account has no passkey yet. Only W doors are
added this way. The new W field is appended to the container. The
existing text is kept byte for byte. A container that already holds a
W door for the passkey of the current account stays unchanged.

Removing a W door needs no secret: the kittygen dialog deletes the W
field from the container text. It refuses to remove the last door of
a container. The R door, the H door and the printed secret cannot be
removed in that dialog.

\S{ppk-hello-moving} Copies, other computers and a lost sidecar

\b A copied or moved protected key needs its sidecar beside it, with
the file name of the new key file plus \c{.hello}. The container is
not bound to a path or to the content of the PPK file. When kageant
copies a protected key file, it copies the sidecar with it. If the
sidecar does not copy, kageant deletes the key copy it just made.

\b On another computer or for another Windows account, the W and H
doors of a sidecar do not open. The passkey of a W door belongs to the
account and computer that created it. The KiTTY++ source documents the
key credential of an H door as reproducible only on the computer and
account that hold it. The R door and the printed secret open the key
there. A W door for that account can then be added
(\k{ppk-hello-doors}).

\b The KiTTY++ source documents the R door as the door that survives
the loss of the computer, a new Windows Hello enrolment or a reset of
the TPM. The printed secret survives these as well.

\b Without its sidecar, or with an unreadable one, the key file is an
ordinary encrypted PPK file. Only the printed secret opens it. A
recovery passphrase or recovery code has no use without the sidecar.

\b When kittygen saves a key without Windows Hello protection over a
protected key file, it deletes the sidecar of that file.

\S{ppk-hello-cli} kittygen-cli

The kittygen-cli option \c{\-\-hello-recovery} \e{file} saves the
output key protected. The recovery passphrase is read from \e{file} and must
not be empty. kittygen-cli writes a sidecar with a single R door and
shows no Windows Hello prompt. It writes the printed secret to
standard output once. It refuses \c{\-\-new-passphrase} together with
this option. W doors can be added later on a computer with Windows
Hello (\k{ppk-hello-doors}).

\S{ppk-hello-versions} Versions

\cq{HELLOK1} is the only container version. The fixed H challenge
string and the fixed W salt belong to this version: the KiTTY++
source requires a new marker for a change to either. Readers ignore
unknown field kinds and unknown trailing tags on R fields, so later
additions can stay within this version. Recovery codes written by
earlier KiTTY++ builds used the printed-secret format instead of the
\cq{KRC1} form. Readers still accept them (\k{ppk-hello-open}).

\H{ppk-old} Older versions of the PPK format

\S{ppk-v2} Version 2

PPK version 2 was used by PuTTY 0.52 to 0.74 inclusive.

In PPK version 2, the MAC algorithm used was HMAC-SHA-1 (so the
\cw{Private-MAC} line contained only 40 hex digits).

The \cq{Key-Derivation:} header and all the
\q{\cw{Argon2-}\e{Parameter}\cw{:}} headers were absent. Instead of
using Argon2, the key material for encrypting the private blob was
derived from the passphrase in a totally different way, as follows.

The cipher key for \cq{aes256-cbc} was constructed by generating two
SHA-1 hashes, concatenating them, and taking the first 32 bytes of the
result. (So you'd get all 20 bytes of the first hash output, and the
first 12 of the second). Each hash preimage was as follows:

\b \cw{uint32}: a sequence number. This is 0 in the first hash, and 1
in the second. (The idea was to extend this mechanism to further
hashes by continuing to increment the sequence number, if future
changes required even longer keys.)

\b the passphrase, without any prefix length field.

In PPK v2, the CBC initialisation vector was all zeroes.

The MAC key was 20 bytes long, and was a single SHA-1 hash of the
following data:

\b the fixed string \cq{putty-private-key-file-mac-key}, without any
prefix length field.

\b the passphrase, without any prefix length field. (If the key is
stored unencrypted, the passphrase was taken to be the empty string
for these purposes.)

\S{ppk-v1} Version 1

PPK version 1 was a badly designed format, only used during initial
development, and not recommended for production use.

PPK version 1 was never used by a released version of PuTTY. It was
only emitted by some early development snapshots between version 0.51
(which did not support SSH-2 public keys at all) and 0.52 (which
already used version 2 of this file format). I \e{hope} there are no
PPK v1 files in use anywhere. But just in case, the old badly designed
format is documented here anyway.

In PPK version 1, the input to the MAC does not include any of the
header fields or the public key. It is simply the private key data
(still in plaintext and including random padding), all by itself
(without a wrapping \cw{string}).

PPK version 1 keys must therefore be rigorously validated after
loading, to ensure that the public and private parts of the key were
consistent with each other.

PPK version 1 only supported the RSA and DSA key types. For RSA, this
validation can be done using only the provided data (since the private
key blob contains enough information to reconstruct the public values
anyway). But for DSA, that isn't quite enough.

Hence, PPK version 1 DSA keys extended the private data so that
immediately after \e{x} was stored an extra value:

\b \cw{string}: a SHA-1 hash of the public key data, whose preimage
consists of

\lcont{

\b \cw{string}: the large prime \e{p}

\b \cw{string}: the small prime \e{q}

\b \cw{string}: the group generator \e{g}

}

The idea was that checking this hash would verify that the key
parameters had not been tampered with, and then the loading
application could directly verify that
\e{g}\cw{^}\e{x}\cw{\_=\_}\e{y}.

In an \e{unencrypted} version 1 key file, the MAC is replaced by a
plain SHA-1 hash of the private key data. This is indicated by the
\cq{Private-MAC:} header being replaced with \cq{Private-Hash:}
instead.
